            <div xml:id="d254">

                <pb facs="0258" n="248" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg248"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.5151/768: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Weddell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-31T13:00:00-03:00">December 31, 1937—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 1:21 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>249. Referring to the Consul’s telegram of December 31, 2 a.m., he now informs me
                    that he was informally advised last night by a member of the Argentine
                    Inter-Ministerial Commission that the latter had just informed the Argentine
                    Ambassador in Washington by telephone that the Argentine Government is prepared,
                    immediately following the preliminary announcement of negotiations, to grant
                    complete official exchange coverage for all United States merchandise with the
                    understanding that the trade agreement to be concluded shall provide a
                    reasonable prospect of the creation of a “satisfactory” amount of dollar
                    exchange.</p>
                <p>The Consul’s telegram above referred to should be read in the light of the
                    present telegram, which he has seen.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Weddell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
